WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.398 --> 00:00:02.238
What happens with open norms?

2
00:00:03.355 --> 00:00:06.555
We propose that nothing
changes from today.

3
00:00:08.098 --> 00:00:11.240
We deal with open norms
in at least two ways.

4
00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:16.880
First, the statute may leave the norm
open, but implementation policy still makes

5
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:17.480
choices.

6
00:00:18.302 --> 00:00:20.864
Or perhaps lower-level legislation.

7
00:00:20.864 --> 00:00:25.944
Sometimes a choice only gets made in a
work instruction, or even in software, and

8
00:00:25.944 --> 00:00:28.609
that closes the open norm.

9
00:00:28.609 --> 00:00:32.932
And what we propose with RegelRecht
is that we don't change that.

10
00:00:32.932 --> 00:00:37.092
The only difference is that we make
legislation and implementation policy

11
00:00:37.092 --> 00:00:38.932
machine-executable.

12
00:00:41.476 --> 00:00:46.756
In other words, we make all those choices
that fill in open norms explicit and

13
00:00:46.756 --> 00:00:47.476
transparent.

14
00:00:50.879 --> 00:00:55.519
That makes it traceable how those
choices were made, and reproducible.

15
00:00:58.441 --> 00:01:03.001
The other kind of open norm is where a
case handler has room to make a choice.

16
00:01:11.169 --> 00:01:13.169
And we don't change that either.

17
00:01:14.211 --> 00:01:19.211
An example in asylum law is the partner
concept, and it can be very hard to prove

18
00:01:19.211 --> 00:01:20.531
you are partners.

19
00:01:28.478 --> 00:01:32.078
For instance, if you can't
marry in your country of origin.

20
00:01:32.921 --> 00:01:37.244
The way it works now is that
evidence has to be provided.

21
00:01:37.244 --> 00:01:40.764
Holiday photos, for example,
could count as evidence.

22
00:01:40.764 --> 00:01:45.724
Then a civil servant assesses them and
concludes it's plausible that two people are

23
00:01:45.724 --> 00:01:46.284
partners.

24
00:01:57.203 --> 00:01:58.723
That works the same.

25
00:01:59.544 --> 00:02:02.104
That judgement becomes a data point.

26
00:02:03.424 --> 00:02:05.584
That a partner is plausible.

27
00:02:07.885 --> 00:02:12.746
And that data point then feeds into
the legislation and regulations.

28
00:02:12.746 --> 00:02:17.706
But reaching that data point, the human
judgement it takes, we don't change.

29
00:02:19.628 --> 00:02:20.771
Still the case.
